@domenic wrote
> As discussed in the thread you link to, we cannot remove the outline algorithm, as _documents still have outlines_. It instead needs to be based on heading levels, without sectioning complications.

additional commits add simplified version of outline in @annevk's PR https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/3499

@domenic 

> Could you also make sure the build passes?

Thanks to @sideshowbarker  build now passes

@myakura @SaekiTominaga 

> Ah good point. @stevefaulkner ?

working on it ;-)

@Alohci wrote:

> Is it worth saying something about nav elements? I'm willing to bet that the vast majority of nav elements in the wild don't have headings and I'm not sure there's value in having advice that's so widely ignored.

I don't disagree, but suggest holding off until this PR lands (or doesn't) in its current form

@zcorpan I pushed two commits. One incorporates your feedback, making the "sectioning root" concept consistent. The second then goes back and removes "sectioning root" concept, replacing any references to it with references to the body element.

I personally found the idea of a category with only one element in it a bit silly. So I think the version after both commits is better. But if I was missing something, we can easily revert the second commit. What do you think?

I will also add back the section on exposing page outlines/headings to users to aid in navigation, since I haven't heard from anyone else on why we should remove that. If there are reasons that weren't expressed we can definitely do so later.
